---
import StarlightPage from "@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro";
import { REPO } from "../lib/agent-metadata";

// There is exactly one channel, and until this page existed the site never said so. A
// reader looking for a contact address found nothing and had to guess whether the project
// was abandoned. Saying "issues only, one maintainer, no SLA" is a better answer than
// silence, and an honest one.
const issues = `${REPO}/issues`;
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: "Contact",
    description:
      "How to report a bug, ask a question, or propose a change to shorn, and what to expect back.",
    tableOfContents: false,
  }}
>
  <p>
    Everything goes through <a href={issues}>GitHub issues</a>. That is the whole contact
    surface: there is no email address, no chat room, no forum, and no support portal. One
    public thread per topic means the next person with the same question can find the
    answer, which a private message would not give them.
  </p>

  <h2>Reporting a bug</h2>

  <p>
    <a href={issues}>Open an issue</a> and include the schema that reproduces it, the value
    you passed, what you expected, and what happened instead. Add the version of shorn, of
    your validator, and of Node. A schema plus a value is usually enough to reproduce
    something exactly, so a small runnable snippet is worth more than a description of it.
    If the bug is that a payload no longer decodes, say which version wrote it.
  </p>

  <h2>Asking a question</h2>

  <p>
    Also an issue. Before you open one, the docs may already answer it: the API reference
    covers every export, the wire format pages describe the bytes field by field, and the
    performance pages give the measurements and their caveats. If the answer was in the
    docs but hard to find, that is still worth an issue, because it is a documentation bug.
  </p>

  <h2>Proposing a change</h2>

  <p>
    Open an issue before writing much code, especially if the change would alter the bytes
    a schema produces. The wire format is a compatibility promise: payloads carry no field
    names and no version, so changing the layout breaks everything already written by an
    earlier release. That conversation is cheaper before the patch than after it.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you want to send a pull request, <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/AGENTS.md`}
      >AGENTS.md</a
    > in the repository covers setup, the layout of the code, the one command that has to be
    green, and what a change needs to say about itself.
  </p>

  <h2>What to expect</h2>

  <p>
    shorn is maintained by one person in their spare time. Replies are best effort, there is
    no response time commitment, and no support contract exists to buy. An issue may sit for
    a while before it gets an answer. Bugs that produce wrong bytes or that break decoding
    come first, since those are the ones that can damage stored data.
  </p>
</StarlightPage>
